Package: mos-wifi
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: wpasupplicant, rfkill, wireless-regdb, iw
Description: mos Wi-Fi station support
 Masks wpa_supplicant.service and its D-Bus activation alias
 dbus-fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1.service by symlinking both to /dev/null, and ships
 the etc-wpa_supplicant.mount that binds /etc/wpa_supplicant from the STATE
 partition together with its local-fs.target.wants link.
 .
 wpa_supplicant@.service is left INSTALLED AND UNENABLED. mosd's station
 reconciler starts and stops one instance per interface and renders that
 instance's configuration file; a distribution unit that also claimed the
 daemon would race it for the same control socket.
 .
 The bind mount is not a convenience. On the mos image /etc is inside the
 read-only dm-verity squashfs, so without it the reconciler's write fails on
 the device and nowhere else, and the PSKs it writes would not survive an A/B
 update.
 .
 Access-point support is mos-wifi-ap, a separate package: this one ships no
 hostapd mask and does not depend on hostapd, so a station-only board does not
 install it. rfkill is required because the radio is soft-blocked out of the
 box on the boards this image targets.
 .
 wireless-regdb is the cfg80211 regulatory database, and iw is what loads it.
 Both are required rather than recommended, and the second is not obvious: the
 board kernels build cfg80211 in and boot with no initramfs, so the kernel's
 own boot-time request for regulatory.db runs before the root is mounted --
 measured on cx3576 as 7.668 s against a root mounted at 7.681 s -- and
 net/wireless/reg.c records that failure in a file-scope pointer that no later
 request consults. Registering a wiphy does not re-read it. The only path that
 clears it is nl80211's RELOAD_REGDB, which is what the
 mos-regdb-reload.service shipped here sends through iw. Without both, a
 country_code rendered into a wpa_supplicant or hostapd instance selects
 channels and transmit power from cfg80211's built-in world domain.
 .
 Architecture: all -- the payload is one unit file and three symlinks, and it
 carries no ELF whose shared-library dependencies a packer could resolve.
